## Migration Analysis

Your code has three patterns that need migration:

| Pattern | Location | Replacement |
|---------|----------|-------------|
| **Cancellable work loop** | `while (true)` in `RunLoop` | `CancellationToken` loop check |
| **Blocking call interruption** | `ManualResetEvent.WaitOne()` | `WaitHandle.WaitAny` with `token.WaitHandle` |
| **Thread.Interrupt + Thread.Abort** | `Stop()` | `CancellationTokenSource.Cancel()` |

## Migrated Code

```csharp
public class EventProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private readonly ManualResetEvent _newEvent = new ManualResetEvent(false);
    private readonly Queue<string> _queue = new Queue<string>();
    private readonly object _lock = new object();
    private readonly CancellationTokenSource _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
    private Thread? _thread;

    public void Start()
    {
        _thread = new Thread(RunLoop);
        _thread.Start();
    }

    private void RunLoop()
    {
        var handles = new WaitHandle[] { _newEvent, _cts.Token.WaitHandle };

        while (!_cts.Token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            // WaitAny returns the index of the handle that was signaled.
            // Index 0 = _newEvent (work available), Index 1 = cancellation requested.
            int signaled = WaitHandle.WaitAny(handles);

            if (signaled == 1 || _cts.Token.IsCancellationRequested)
                break;

            _newEvent.Reset();

            string? item = null;
            lock (_lock)
            {
                if (_queue.Count > 0)
                    item = _queue.Dequeue();
            }

            if (item is not null)
                Handle(item);
        }
    }

    public void Enqueue(string item)
    {
        lock (_lock) { _queue.Enqueue(item); }
        _newEvent.Set();
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        _cts.Cancel();           // wakes RunLoop via WaitHandle.WaitAny
        _thread?.Join(5000);     // wait up to 5s for clean exit
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _cts.Dispose();
        _newEvent.Dispose();
    }
}
```

## Key Changes

1. **`Thread.Abort()` / `Thread.Interrupt()` → `CancellationTokenSource.Cancel()`** — cooperative cancellation replaces forced termination. The runtime no longer supports `Thread.Abort` (`PlatformNotSupportedException` / `SYSLIB0006`).

2. **`_newEvent.WaitOne()` → `WaitHandle.WaitAny(handles)`** — the wait now responds to *both* new work and cancellation. Without this, the thread stays blocked forever since `Cancel()` can't interrupt `WaitOne()`.

3. **`while (true)` → `while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)`** — the loop voluntarily exits when cancellation is requested.

4. **Added `IDisposable`** — `CancellationTokenSource` leaks timers/callbacks if not disposed.

## Pitfalls to Watch

| Pitfall | Why it matters |
|---------|---------------|
| **Keeping `_newEvent.WaitOne()` without `WaitAny`** | `Cancel()` won't wake the thread — it stays blocked indefinitely. You *must* include `token.WaitHandle` in the wait. |
| **Race: multiple items queued, only one dequeued** | The original code had this bug too — `Set()`/`Reset()` can collapse multiple signals. The migrated code adds a `Count > 0` guard to avoid `InvalidOperationException`. Consider draining the queue in a loop. |
| **Not disposing `CancellationTokenSource`** | Causes timer and callback leaks. Always wrap in `using` or implement `IDisposable`. |
| **Expecting `Cancel()` to be instant** | Cooperative cancellation only fires at the next checkpoint (`WaitAny` / `IsCancellationRequested`). If `Handle(item)` is long-running, pass the token into it and check periodically. |
| **Removing `Thread.Join` timeout** | Keep the 5-second join. If `Handle()` ignores cancellation, you still need a bounded wait so `Stop()` doesn't hang forever. |
